# The DisjointMut wide path at t=1: eliminated, measured, and NOT shipped on

date: 2026-08-08
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P + 4 E = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: stable, release (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), DEFAULT features
       (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut). No `-C target-cpu=native`.
base: `main` @ fc120a8, 766/766 dav1d-test-data.
branch: `perf/shard-mapping`, sibling worktree `rav1d-safe--shardmap`.
vectors: `~/tmp/rav1d-perf/vec/*.avif`.
raw: `benchmarks/tracker_shardrun_2026-08-08.tsv`
     (campaign, vector, bpc, threads, round, arm, ms/frame, frame md5, foreign).

**THE BOX WAS NOT IDLE.** Two other agents held a core each for the whole
session (`zenav1-svt/.../identity_run`, `bpcshift/bin/bench_base`), so 194 of
the 217 raw rows record a foreign process over 25% CPU. Every cell is
`scripts/perf/st1_ab.sh`: arms interleaved back to back with a rotating order
inside each round, so both arms of a comparison see the same load and the
RATIO survives. **Absolute ms/frame here is inflated and is not comparable to
an idle campaign; read ratios only.** Min and max are printed for every arm so
the noise band is visible rather than asserted.

NO `nice` ON ANY TIMED RUN (Darwin maps it to background QoS, ~40x distortion).
Builds and the correctness sweep were niced.

---

## 1. The diagnosis, confirmed with the counters before any code was written

`examples/probe_tracker --features probe-wide`, v4k_8tile, t=1, **per 10
frames** (`WIDE_FULL` and `WIDE_BLOCKS` are exactly zero in every row below, so
every promotion came through the `WIDE_SHARDS` door — more than
`MAX_SHARDS_PER_BORROW = 4` distinct shards):

    cell              add_slow    add_multi    WIDE_SHARDS
    -----------------------------------------------------
    8bpc   t=1       6,204,930    6,204,930      5,033,100
    8bpc   t=8          57,553       57,553              0
    10bpc  t=1       3,349,700    3,349,700      3,111,250
    10bpc  t=8          59,704       59,704              0

503,310 wide borrows per frame at 8bpc t=1, each locking and scanning all 32
active shards on registration and locking them all again on release. The t=8
rows reproduce `tracker_blockshift_2026-08-08.meta` §3's "zero at every shift":
tile threading sends `block_mut` down the per-row path, so this is a t=1-only
problem, exactly as `st1_kernels_2026-08-08.meta` §1 said.

Mechanism: `WithOffset::block_mut` / `narrow_guard(w, h)` reserve the strided
HULL `(h-1)*stride + w`. An 8x8 transform at a 3840-byte 4K stride is 26,888
bytes = 7 blocks at `BLOCK_SHIFT` 12, hence up to 7 shards. Only 4x4 stays
narrow.

## 2. The named candidate is a re-parameterisation, not a new mechanism

The brief proposed `shard_of(block) = (block >> run_log2) & mask` to keep a run
of consecutive blocks on one shard. With the fast path's single-shard test read
at the same run granularity — which strictly dominates, since a borrow inside
one run genuinely is one shard and should take the one-lock path — that is
**identical** to raising the shift by `run_log2`:

    shard = hash((addr >> S) >> R)  ==  hash(addr >> (S+R))

`shift` has no other consumer: it appears only in `add`'s `b0 != b1` test, in
`add_slow`'s repeat of it, and in `add_multi`'s block enumeration. So the
deliverable is a *policy* over granularity, not a second mechanism, and it is
implemented as one — `serial_shift_rule` in `tracker_shard.rs`. Keeping a
separate `run` field would have been dead weight.

The fixed ladder (a rung compiled in, so every instance takes it), same probe,
per 10 frames, t=1:

    shift   8bpc add_multi   8bpc WIDE   10bpc add_multi   10bpc WIDE
       12        6,204,930   5,033,100         3,349,700    3,111,250
       13        6,137,520   1,856,890         3,247,460    2,254,620
       14        5,772,350     351,340         3,178,140      846,050
       15        4,617,580           0         2,876,040      170,670
       16        2,566,560           0         2,214,750            0

8bpc needs 15, 10bpc needs 16 — one rung apart because a 10-bit plane has twice
the stride. `size_shift(len)` already tracks that (it lands both depths on the
same picture-rows-per-block), so the shipped rule is ONE bump on top of it:

    serial_shift_rule(len) = clamp(size_shift(len) + SERIAL_RUN, BLOCK_SHIFT, 24)

The `BLOCK_SHIFT` floor is load-bearing: it stops a small instance being turned
into one block and one lock, which is what a fixed rung of 15 would do. With
`SERIAL_RUN = 1` the wide count goes to **ZERO at both depths** (measured, not
derived: 5,033,100 -> 0 and 3,111,250 -> 0), with `WIDE_FULL` still 0, while
the t=8 counters are unchanged.

## 3. Wall clock — and why this ships OFF

Median of 7 (5 for the t>1 rows), interleaved, rotating order. Every arm in
every cell produced the SAME frame md5, so all of this is bit-identical.
Arm -> effective shift on the 4K luma plane: 8bpc `m2`=12 `m1`=13 `r0`=14
`r1`=15 `r2`=16 `r3`=17; 10bpc one higher throughout.

    v4k_8tile 8bpc t=1        median     min      max    ratio
    off  (shipped default)    406.51  403.24   416.66   1.0000
    r0                        395.73  389.58   402.18   0.9735
    r1                        383.86  381.10   392.59   0.9443
    r2                        381.01  378.90   391.10   0.9373
    r3                        380.52  377.12   388.73   0.9361

    v4k_8tile_10b t=1         median     min      max    ratio
    off                       441.94  438.13   445.76   1.0000
    r0                        464.20  460.74   467.46   1.0504
    r1                        463.36  457.23   466.69   1.0485
    r2                        460.89  457.42   467.93   1.0429
    r3                        461.28  457.10   467.41   1.0438

    v4k_8tile_10b t=1 (second campaign, adds the sub-size-rule rungs)
    off                       452.87  445.48   462.73   1.0000
    m2                        457.76  440.49   474.31   1.0108
    m1                        478.76  464.43   509.96   1.0572
    r0                        478.52  467.73   509.68   1.0566
    r1                        473.00  464.14   478.28   1.0445

8bpc min/max bands do not overlap (off 403.24-416.66 vs r1 381.10-392.59), and
r0/r1 reproduce across the two independent 10bpc campaigns. **-5.6% at 8bpc and
+4.9% at 10bpc, both real.**

Generality of the 8bpc win, and the shape of the cost:

    cell                        off      r1 ratio   r2 ratio
    v4k_1tile   8bpc  t=1    408.71      0.9434     0.9429
    v1024       8bpc  t=1     30.88      1.0091     0.9984
    v256        8bpc  t=1      0.97      1.0026     1.0009

The win is a 4K phenomenon and the mechanism says why: at a 1024-byte stride an
8x8 hull is 7,176 bytes = 2 blocks, already narrow, so there is nothing to fix
and nothing to lose. Small sizes are null in both directions.

t>1 is untouched, which is the point of gating on declared parallelism:

    v4k_8tile     8bpc t=4   off 126.42   r1 0.9917  (n=5)
    v4k_8tile     8bpc t=8   off  79.35   r1 0.9944  (n=5)
    v4k_8tile_10b     t=8    off  89.03   r1 1.0132  (n=9, bands overlap: off
                                                      84.72-93.07, r1 85.18-91.36)

The 10bpc t=8 cell first read 1.0223 at n=5 and collapsed to null at n=9 — a
worked example of the brief's "check the noise band before believing a sub-3%
claim". It is reported as NULL.

### Why 10bpc loses, from the profile rather than from a story

`/usr/bin/sample`, 45 s of steady state, self time per leaf
(`scripts/perf/sample_selftime.py`), v4k_8tile_10b t=1, off vs r1. Converted to
per-frame cost (share x ms/frame, so the differing frame counts cancel):

    symbol                                   change in per-frame cost
    decode_coefs (46% of wall, no tracker)          +0.9%   <- flat: control
    BorrowTracker::add<true>                       +53%
    BorrowTracker::add<false>                      +41%
    add_wide<false>                               -100%     (14.8 -> 0 ms)
    remove_wide                                   -100%      (8.1 -> 0 ms)
    DisjointMut::slice_as::<u16>                   +27%
    LfBlock::open                                  +23%
    rav1d_recon_b_intra                            +15%

The wide path really is gone; `add`'s fast path got more expensive by slightly
more than the wide path saved. `add` is `#[inline]`, which is why its callers
(`slice_as`, `LfBlock::open`, `recon_b_intra`) grew too while `decode_coefs`
stayed flat. The cost is `ShardRecs::find`'s scan: at a granularity coarse
enough to keep an `h`-row hull inside 4 blocks, borrows one ROW apart
necessarily land in the same block and therefore the same shard, so per-row
borrows that used to be alone on a shard now scan each other.

**That tension is irreducible for any mapping.** To put a span of `L`
consecutive blocks on <= 4 shards the mapping must be constant on runs of ~L/4
blocks; per-row borrows are one stride apart and fall inside such a run. Adding
shards does not help — the colliding borrows are in the SAME block. The 16-bit
path simply keeps more borrows live at once (`slice_as::<u16>` /
`mut_slice_as::<u16>` are 17.4 ms/frame at 10bpc and do not exist at 8bpc), so
it pays the collision and 8bpc does not.

There is no rung that serves both: matched at ~4.3 picture rows per block, 8bpc
is 0.9735 and 10bpc is 1.0566. The discriminator is the pixel size, which
`BorrowTracker::new(len)` cannot see — a 4K 8-bit plane and a 4K 10-bit plane
differ only by a factor of two in `len`, which an 8K 8-bit plane also does.

**So the default is `SERIAL_RUN = None`, byte-for-byte the behaviour `main`
shipped**, pinned by `the_serial_rule_is_off_by_default`. Turning it on
requires one new input, and it is the input
`st1_kernels_2026-08-08.meta` §4 item 1 already asked for: the pixel stride or
`BD::BPC` at tracker construction (`Rav1dPictureDataComponent::from_parts` has
the stride in hand). With that, `shardrun-1` is a -5.6% t=1 win on 8-bit 4K at
zero cost elsewhere, and the numbers above are the whole A/B, so it can be
flipped without re-measuring.

## 4. What is NOT done, and the next lever

* **Not shipped enabled.** The mechanism is proven and measured; the gate that
  makes it safe is not built.
* **Not measured: 12bpc.** `BitDepth16` covers 10 and 12; only 10 was run.
* **Not measured: any non-4K 10bpc cell, and any t=2.**
* **A self-tuning shift (measure the promotion rate, adapt) is UNSOUND** and
  must not be attempted: both registrants of a shared byte have to agree on the
  block boundary, so the granularity may only move in `reprovision(&mut self)`.
* **The better lever, untried: stop reserving the hull.** The wide path exists
  because a `w x h` block borrow reserves `(h-1)*stride + w` to touch `w*h`
  pixels. Splitting it into row-GROUPS of <= 4 blocks each would keep every
  piece on the multi-shard fast path at TODAY's fine granularity — removing the
  wide path with no collision cost, hence at both bit depths. That is a
  `BlockMut`/kernel-API change in `src/`, not a tracker change, and it is where
  the remaining 41 ms/frame at 8bpc t=1 lives.

## 5. Correctness

* `cargo test --release --test decode_md5_verify`: 14/14 on the shipped default
  AND on `--features shardrun-1`.
* `examples/md5_inventory` on `shardrun-1`: **766 PASS, 2 SKIP**, and a set-diff
  BY NAME (group, name -> status, actual hash) against
  `benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst` is **0 differences,
  0 vectors added, 0 removed**.
* Every A/B arm in every cell emitted the same frame md5 as its comparand.
* `cargo test -p rav1d-disjoint-mut`: 26 lib + 25 soundness + 5 shard_liveness
  + 1 wide_exclusion, green on the default and on `__shardrun_1`.

### The soundness gate was proven live, twice, and its blind spot confirmed

Granularity is not a correctness knob — "no missed overlap" needs only that
both registrants of a shared byte agree on the block boundary, and the value is
fixed for a tracker's life — but the claim was tested rather than asserted:

* Hazard (a), the 4af62ae TOCTOU shape (delete `add`'s in-lock `state`
  re-read): `wide_exclusion::a_wide_borrow_excludes_every_narrow_shard` FAILED
  in 0.01 s with 123 witnesses.
* Hazard (b), `active()` shortened by one shard: FAILED in 0.01 s with 1
  witness.
* Restored from a byte-level snapshot both times (`diff` clean), green again.
* **Confirmed: with hazard (a) planted, `soundness.rs` (25 tests) and
  `shard_liveness.rs` (5 tests) BOTH PASS.** `wide_exclusion.rs` is the only
  gate for that shape.
* `the_serial_rule_clears_the_wide_path_at_both_depths` was mutation-tested
  (neuter the rule to return `BLOCK_SHIFT`): FAILS, restored, green.
